ructive, not rejection-based**: Generation logic must directly construct objects that satisfy constraints, not randomly generate candidates and filter. For example, if a sprite needs a 2-pixel contact edge, grow it from those 2 pixels — don't generate random sprites and hope one matches. The only acceptable rejection loop is the outer `_gen_attempt` retry + final `main(inp) == out` oracle check. Any inner loop with > 50% rejection rate means the approach is wrong — restructure to construct the desired output directly.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:69:3. **Output-first means output-first in code structure**: When the generation_strategy in spec says \"output-first\" approach, your code must literally follow the generation_strategy steps to build the full output first (typically the more constrained structure), then derive the input (the easier thing) from it by undoing the solver's transformation described in `main()`. Finally, validate with `np.array_equal(main(input), output)`. You are doing it wrong if your code places input objects first and hopes the solver produces a good output by random trial, which often has near-zero acceptance rate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:72:6. **Code structure**: split into `_gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None` (single attempt, returns grid or None on failure) and `generate_input()` (retry loop, max 300 attempts). This keeps generation logic flat and readable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:90:2. **Absolute constraints** (e.g. `count: 3`, `single connected component`, `no overlap`): extract ALL non-`[observed]` absolute constraints from the spec, verify that **100% of samples** satisfy each one. Flag any violation. These are hard requirements — even a single violation means the generator is buggy.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:106:**When `attempt_i` is provided (best-of-N mode)**: Do NOT modify the spec file. Instead, note any suggested spec changes in your self-check report for the parent to review after all attempts complete.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:115:- If the solver assumes every object participates in the transformation, verify all generated objects are reachable/active.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:119:Append `generate_input()` (and any helper functions it needs) to `/home/xd/projects/BARC/solvers/arc2/<task_id>.py` (or to the temp file if `attempt_i` was provided).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:10:1. Guide a separate LLM agent to write a `generate_input()` function\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:11:2. Allow a **human** to quickly review and correct the spec before generation\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:23:- A generator author can implement `generate_input()` purely from this spec\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:24:- A reviewer can verify generated data quality by checking against this spec\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:45:**Self-check**: verify that every non-`[observed]` constraint in `spec:` can be satisfied by the `generation_strategy:` steps — either constructively (guaranteed by a specific step) or with low rejection rate. If a constraint would require high rejection rate, revise the strategy to satisfy it constructively (e.g. switch to output-first, or add a constructive placement step).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:47:Aim for **≤40 lines** total. The spec should be concise enough for a human to review at a glance. If the spec is significantly longer than 40 lines, try to condense.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/skills/gen-variant-code/SKILL.md:7:对用户确认的创新变体题目，生成变体实现代码（包含 `main()` 和 `generate_input()`），测试验证，输出汇总报告。变体列表从 